

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Second question — this is the one that decides whether the module actually delivers "easier to change environments."

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether switching environments means [3mediting a value[0m or [3mthe code choosing for you at runtime[0m. That shapes
  the config's stored shape, so it's the part worth getting right up front; adding an override later on top of a map is
  cheap, but flattening a map back out after code depends on it isn't.

  - [1mSingle flat config, edit to switch.[0m One object, one endpoint. Cheapest, and honest about a fixture-sized app. Costs:
    switching is still a hand edit, just a tidier one — arguably no better than today.
  - [1mNamed environment map + one active-env constant.[0m [38;5;153mENVIRONMENTS = { development, staging, production }[39m and a single
    line you flip. Buys: all environments visible and diffable in one place, which is usually what "easier to change
    environments" means. Costs: you still edit a file to switch, and the extra indirection is only worth it with 2+ real
    environments.
  - [1mAuto-detect from [38;5;153mlocation.hostname[39m.[0m [38;5;153mlocalhost[39m → dev, anything else → production, no edit at all. Buys: never ship
    the wrong endpoint by forgetting to flip a constant. Costs: implicit behavior, and it needs the env map anyway — so
    it's the map plus a resolver, not an alternative to it.

  [1mMy recommendation: the environment map with an explicit active-env constant, and hostname detection as the default [0m
  [1mvalue of that constant.[0m You get the no-edit common case without the magic being unoverridable — the constant stays
  there to force a specific environment when you need to. If you'd rather keep it dead simple, the flat config is a
  legitimate call for a repo this size.
